{"questions":[{"question_id":"lsaowhvy","content":"Given below are two statements :<br><br>\n<b>Statement (I)</b> : A <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bonding MO has lower electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis.\n<br><br>\n<b>Statement (II)</b> : The <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> antibonding MO has a node between the nuclei.<br><br>\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the <b>most appropriate</b> answer from the options given below :","chapter":"chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","country":"in","difficulty":"medium","exam":"jee-main","examGroup":"jee","isBonus":false,"isOutOfSyllabus":false,"languages":["en","hi"],"marks":4,"negMarks":1,"paperId":"jee-main-2024-online-1st-february-evening-shift","paperTitle":"JEE Main 2024 (Online) 1st February Evening Shift","permalink":"given-below-are-two-statements-brbr-bstatement-i-jee-main-chemistry-knr8msquh3r3cfmy","question":{"en":{"comprehension":null,"direction":null,"content":"Given below are two statements :<br><br>\n<b>Statement (I)</b> : A <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bonding MO has lower electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis.\n<br><br>\n<b>Statement (II)</b> : The <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> antibonding MO has a node between the nuclei.<br><br>\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the <b>most appropriate</b> answer from the options given below :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Both Statement I and Statement II are true"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Both Statement I and Statement II are false"},{"identifier":"C","content":"Statement I is true but Statement II is false"},{"identifier":"D","content":"Statement I is false but Statement II is true"}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>Let's analyze both statements:</p>\n\n<p><b>Statement (I)</b>: \"A <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bonding MO has lower electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis.\"</p>\n\n<p>This statement is false. In molecular orbital (MO) theory, a pi bond (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bond) is formed by the sideways overlap of p-orbitals from two adjacent atoms. The characteristic electron density of a <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bonding molecular orbital is concentrated above and below the inter-nuclear axis, not lower. These regions of electron density are where the p-orbitals overlap and electrons are likely to be found. This concentration of electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis is what allows the <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bond to provide additional stability to the molecule, alongside any sigma (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.294ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 572 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) bonds that may be present.</p>\n\n<p><b>Statement (II)</b>: \"The <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> antibonding MO has a node between the nuclei.\"</p>\n\n<p>This statement is true. In a <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (pi-star) antibonding molecular orbital, there is indeed a nodal plane between the nuclei where the probability of finding electrons is essentially zero. This node arises from the out-of-phase combination of the p-orbitals from adjacent atoms, which results in destructive interference and a region of zero electron density in the bonding region. The presence of this nodal plane is what makes the <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> orbital antibonding—the electrons in this orbital actually serve to destabilize the bond between the two atoms.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is:</p>\n<p>Option D: Statement I is false but Statement II is true.</p>"},"hi":{"direction":null,"comprehension":null,"content":"<p>नीचे दो कथन दिए गए हैं :</p>\n<p>कथन (I) : किसी <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> आबंधी अणु कक्षक में अंतरानाभिक अक्ष के ऊपर और नीचे इलेक्ट्रॉन घनत्व कम होता है।</p>\n<p>कथन (II) : <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> अनाबंधी अणु कक्षक में नाभिकों के बीच एक नोड होती है।</p>\n<p>ऊपर दिए गए कथनों के संदर्भ में, नीचे दिए गए विकल्पों में सही उत्तर चुनिए :</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"कथन I और कथन II दोनों सत्य हैं।\n\n"},{"identifier":"B","content":"कथन I और कथन II दोनों असत्य हैं।"},{"identifier":"C","content":"कथन I सत्य है परंतु कथन II असत्य है।"},{"identifier":"D","content":"कथन I असत्य है परंतु कथन II सत्य है।"}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>आइए दोनों कथनों का विश्लेषण करें:</p>\n\n<p><b>कथन (I)</b>: \"एक <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> बंधन MO में अंतरानाभिकीय अक्ष के ऊपर और नीचे इलेक्ट्रॉन घनत्व कम होता है।\"</p>\n\n<p>यह कथन गलत है। आणविक कक्षक (MO) सिद्धांत में, पाई बंधन (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> बंधन) दो आसन्न परमाणुओं के p-कक्षकों के पार्श्विक अतिव्यापन द्वारा बनता है। एक <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> बंधन आणविक कक्षक की विशेषता यह है कि इलेक्ट्रॉन घनत्व अंतरानाभिकीय अक्ष के ऊपर और नीचे केंद्रित होता है, कम नहीं। इलेक्ट्रॉन घनत्व के ये क्षेत्र वे स्थान हैं जहां p-कक्षक अतिव्यापित होते हैं और इलेक्ट्रॉन पाए जाने की संभावना होती है। अंतरानाभिकीय अक्ष के ऊपर और नीचे इलेक्ट्रॉन घनत्व की यह सघनता ही वह है जो <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> बंधन को अणु में अतिरिक्त स्थिरता प्रदान करने की अनुमति देती है, किसी भी सिग्मा (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.294ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 572 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) बंधन के साथ जो उपस्थित हो सकते हैं।</p>\n\n<p><b>कथन (II)</b>: \"<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> प्रतिबंधन MO में नाभिकों के बीच एक निस्पंद होता है।\"</p>\n\n<p>यह कथन सत्य है। एक <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (पाई-स्टार) प्रतिबंधन आणविक कक्षक में, वास्तव में नाभिकों के बीच एक निस्पंद तल होता है जहां इलेक्ट्रॉन मिलने की प्रायिकता अनिवार्य रूप से शून्य होती है। यह निस्पंद आसन्न परमाणुओं के p-कक्षकों के विपरीत-कला संयोजन से उत्पन्न होता है, जिसके परिणामस्वरूप विनाशकारी व्यतिकरण होता है और बंधन क्षेत्र में शून्य इलेक्ट्रॉन घनत्व का एक क्षेत्र बनता है। इस निस्पंद तल की उपस्थिति ही <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> कक्षक को प्रतिबंधन बनाती है—इस कक्षक में इलेक्ट्रॉन वास्तव में दो परमाणुओं के बीच बंधन को अस्थिर करने का कार्य करते हैं।</p>\n\n<p>इसलिए, सही उत्तर है:</p>\n<p>विकल्प D: कथन I गलत है लेकिन कथन II सत्य है।</p>"}},"subject":"chemistry","timeAllotted":120000,"topic":"molecular-orbital-theory","type":"mcq","year":2024,"tags":["assertion_reason"],"yearKey":"jee-main-2024-online-1st-february-evening-shift","active":true,"options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Both Statement I and Statement II are true"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Both Statement I and Statement II are false"},{"identifier":"C","content":"Statement I is true but Statement II is false"},{"identifier":"D","content":"Statement I is false but Statement II is true"}],"correct_options":["D"],"explanation":"<p>Let's analyze both statements:</p>\n\n<p><b>Statement (I)</b>: \"A <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bonding MO has lower electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis.\"</p>\n\n<p>This statement is false. In molecular orbital (MO) theory, a pi bond (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bond) is formed by the sideways overlap of p-orbitals from two adjacent atoms. The characteristic electron density of a <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bonding molecular orbital is concentrated above and below the inter-nuclear axis, not lower. These regions of electron density are where the p-orbitals overlap and electrons are likely to be found. This concentration of electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis is what allows the <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.296ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 573 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> bond to provide additional stability to the molecule, alongside any sigma (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.294ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 572 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) bonds that may be present.</p>\n\n<p><b>Statement (II)</b>: \"The <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> antibonding MO has a node between the nuclei.\"</p>\n\n<p>This statement is true. In a <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (pi-star) antibonding molecular orbital, there is indeed a nodal plane between the nuclei where the probability of finding electrons is essentially zero. This node arises from the out-of-phase combination of the p-orbitals from adjacent atoms, which results in destructive interference and a region of zero electron density in the bonding region. The presence of this nodal plane is what makes the <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.398ex\" height=\"1.59ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -691.8 1059.7 702.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(656.1,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> orbital antibonding—the electrons in this orbital actually serve to destabilize the bond between the two atoms.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is:</p>\n<p>Option D: Statement I is false but Statement II is true.</p>"}]}